38 #edb/ref 17592186335840 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059669 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335841 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335855 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335856 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335857 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335858 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059691 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335739 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059747 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335999 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335996 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335997 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335998 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336018 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336005 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336008 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059769 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335773 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059769 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335864 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335888 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335944 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335968 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059772 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335942 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336022 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059826 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335842 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335848 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335769 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335770 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186059854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336017 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058710 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186060353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335573 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060353 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\frac{\\cos^{2} x}{\\sin x + 1}$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186060353 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060353 1074 "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}=\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1} & =\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058716 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186060354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335552 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060354 1071 "If $\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{\\sin^{2} x} = (1)/(f(x))$, then $f(x) =$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186060354 1071 "If $\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{1}{f(x)},$ then $f(x)=$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060354 1074 "First, we rewrite the denominator using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the denominator as difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058740 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186060358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335281 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060358 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})=$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060358 1071 "What is $\\sin (x - 45^{∘})$?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186060358 1074 "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={45}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058824 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186060372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335541 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060372 1074 "The given curve is the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\nNotice that, because the cosine function is even, it is symmetric about the $y$-axis, i.e.,\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x\n$$\n\nSo the graph could also be viewed as $y=cos⁡(-x).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058896 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186060384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335562 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060384 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060384 1074 "![](images/c2/c27ff316601ece28946ef45711690d6aabca92a4c35f07b0b1cc1c0982fb5d8e.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-cos⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $1$ unit **up** to give $y=-cos⁡x+1$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-cos⁡x+1.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058920 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186060388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335501 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060388 1071 "Let $C = AB$. What is the value of $c_{21}$, if\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186060388 1071 "Let $C=AB.$ What is the value of ${c}_{21}$, if\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060388 1074 "Multiplying the $2$nd row in $A$ by the $1$st column in $B$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058944 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186060392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335390 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186060392 1074 "The coordinates of the point are $(0,-2).$\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=0,$ and\n\n- the imaginary part is $y=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the number depicted is $z=0-2\\text{i},$ which can be simplfied to $z=-2\\text{i}.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186064256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335982 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186064256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335983 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186064938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063654 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186064938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335682 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186064938 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186064938 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int3^{x}dx$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186064938 1071 "Calculate $\\int {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186064938 1074 "To compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186064947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063708 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186064947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335564 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186064947 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186064947 1071 "$\\int \\frac{8\\,\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}=$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186064947 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int(8dx)/(\\mid 8x \\mid \\sqrt{(8x)^{2} - 1})$?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186064947 1074 "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{8\\,\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}=8\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $8x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=8x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}8\\int \\frac{1}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =8\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186068701 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336010 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186069506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186068176 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186069506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335648 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186069506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186069506 1071 "$\\frac{5{b}^{2}}{2}÷\\frac{b}{7}=$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186069506 1071 "What is $\\frac{5b^{2}}{2} \\div \\frac{b}{7}$?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186069506 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{b}^{2}}{2}÷\\frac{b}{7},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{b}^{2}}{2}÷\\frac{b}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{5{b}^{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{b} & = \\\\ \\frac{35{b}^{2}}{2b} & = \\\\ \\frac{35{b}^{\\cancel{2}}}{2\\cancel{b}} & = \\\\ \\frac{35b}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $b$ in the numerator and denominator." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186078422 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335917 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186078439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335925 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186078439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335926 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186078439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335927 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186078439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335929 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186078497 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335933 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186078498 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335935 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186078498 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335936 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186078502 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335743 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186078579 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335963 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186078579 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335964 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186078594 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335787 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186078595 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335786 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186078622 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335955 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186078624 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335957 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186079168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186077756 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186079168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335385 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186079168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186079168 1071 "Given that $y = y(t)$ satisfies the equation $y^{″} + 3y^{′} - 4y = 0$, which of the following **cannot** be $y(t)$?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186079168 1071 "Given that $y=y(t)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(t)?$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186079168 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}-4{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =1.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-4t}$ and ${e}^{t}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $-{e}^{-4t}+{e}^{-t}$ is not a solution." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186079171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186077774 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186079171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335660 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186079171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186079171 1071 "Given that $D = x\\frac{d^{2}}{dx^{2}} + 2\\frac{d}{dx}$, what is $D(y)$ for $y = \\ln x$?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186079171 1071 "Given that $D=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x?$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186079171 1074 "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=2,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =(x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186079192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186077900 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186079192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335246 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186079192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186079192 1071 "Factor the expression $p^{4} - 1$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186079192 1071 "Factor the expression ${p}^{4}-1.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186079192 1074 "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{p}^{4}-1={({p}^{2})}^{2}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{({p}^{2})}^{2}-{1}^{2}=({p}^{2}+1)({p}^{2}-1)\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({p}^{2}+1)({p}^{2}-1) & =({p}^{2}+1)({p}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =({p}^{2}+1)(p+1)(p-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335879 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083159 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335880 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083159 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335884 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083159 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335885 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083159 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335886 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335873 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335881 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335882 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335883 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083161 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335887 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335797 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335805 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335798 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335809 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335832 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335834 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083238 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335908 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335956 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335959 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335969 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083251 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335970 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083312 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335893 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083312 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335900 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083313 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335911 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083386 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335928 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083387 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335932 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083388 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335930 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083388 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335931 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083875 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082151 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083875 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335535 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083875 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083875 1071 "Find the range of the function $f(x) =-\\sec (2x - \\frac{π}{4}) + 1$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083875 1071 "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083875 1074 "The range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $-1$ and adding $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\ge 1+1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $-1$ and adding $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\le -1+1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get \n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 2,\\,f(x)\\le 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082163 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335592 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083877 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083877 1071 "Which expression gives the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x) = \\csc (4x + 1) - 2$? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083877 1071 "Which expression gives the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+1)-2?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083877 1074 "The vertical asymptotes of the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+1)-2,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(4x+1).$ This gives\n\n$$\n4x+1=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+1 & =n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =-1+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4},$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082169 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335517 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083878 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083878 1071 "Calculate the period of the function $y = \\frac{1}{2}\\sec (3x + \\frac{π}{4}) - 1$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083878 1071 "Calculate the period of the function $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})-1.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083878 1074 "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=3.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082181 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335378 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083880 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083880 1071 "Find the range of the function $f(x) = \\frac{1}{3}\\csc (\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{π}{8}) - 1$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083880 1071 "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083880 1074 "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $\\frac{1}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1\\le -\\frac{1}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $\\frac{1}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1\\ge \\frac{1}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge -\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -\\frac{4}{3},\\,f(x)\\ge -\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{4}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082187 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335290 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083881 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083881 1071 "What is the value of $f(x) = \\frac{1}{2}\\sec (3x + \\frac{π}{4}) + 2$ at its local maximum points?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083881 1071 "What is the value of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2$ at its local maximum points?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083881 1074 "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-1$ are local maximums of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ \n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $\\frac{1}{2}$ and adding $2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2\\le -\\frac{1}{2}+2 \\\\  & f(x)\\le \\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}$ at the local maximum points." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082289 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335491 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083898 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x) = 3x^{3} - 12x^{2} - 3x + 12$?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083898 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-3x+12?$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083898 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $12$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }12}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{1}{2}$ does not take the above form. The denominator $(2)$ is not a factor of $3.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082391 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335335 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083915 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083915 1071 "For the parametric curve\n$x = 3\\sin θ, y = 5\\cos θ, 0 \\le θ < 2π$, calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083915 1071 "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\n calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083915 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082397 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335480 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083916 1071 "![](images/17/1739317bf1b1c567f74dccf5b24a1b1dde8eb101d20ceec9652f604dc6f84543.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve\n$x = 4\\cos t, y = 3\\sin 2t, 0 \\le t < 2π$,\nshown above, calculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t = \\frac{π}{6}$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083916 1071 "![](images/17/1739317bf1b1c567f74dccf5b24a1b1dde8eb101d20ceec9652f604dc6f84543.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083916 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /6} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082409 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335453 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083918 1071 "![](images/ed/edb4268917ba8cb82e1bbb2141cb166f8a5960d2bb4d0b511e651f7c13ed4f61.png)\n\nA curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 2t^{3} - 3t, y = 3t^{2} - 5, - ∞ < t < ∞$.\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t = 1$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186083918 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-3t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}-5,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186083918 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(1)^{3}-3(1)=2-3=-1, \\\\ y & =3(1)^{2}-5=3-5=-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(-1,-2).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6t}{6{t}^{2}-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6\\cdot 1}{6\\cdot (1)^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6-3} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,-2)$ and the slope $m=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =2(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186087697 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335924 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186087699 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335988 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186087701 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335972 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186087703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335973 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186087703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335974 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186087704 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335975 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186087719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335984 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186087719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335985 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186087723 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335934 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186087723 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335965