70 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088231 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=5.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088231 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n$y^{″} + 9y = 6\\cos 3x - 12\\sin 3x, y(0) = 2, y^{′}(0) = 5$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186088231 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\pm 3\\text{i},$ and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+3x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ {y}^{″} & =6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ -6\\alpha =-12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3P\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+x(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3Q+2=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P=2,Q=1.$\n\nSo, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086022 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186088233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335329 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088233 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 9y =-6\\cos 3x$\nhas the complementary function $y_{c}(x) = A\\cos 3x + B\\sin 3x$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186088233 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+9y=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088233 1074 "The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+3x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)-9x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)-9x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta =-6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ -6\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =0$ and $\\beta =-1.$ Therefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086040 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186088236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335415 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088236 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 3\\frac{dy}{dx} - 4y = e^{-3x}$\nhas the complementary function $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{x} + Be^{-4x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186088236 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y={e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088236 1074 "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-4x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =-4.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is ${e}^{-3x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-3$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=9\\alpha {e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+3(-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x})-4(\\alpha {e}^{-3x}) & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ (9-9-4)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ -4\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-3x}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086064 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186088240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335512 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088240 1071 "Consider the equation $y^{″} - 6y^{′} + 10y = 0$ where $y = y(t)$. If $y = e^{λt}$ is a solution, then $λ =$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186088240 1071 "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+10y=0$ where $y=y(t).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088240 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+10{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +10) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda =3\\pm \\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086070 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186088241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335574 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088241 1071 "Consider the equation $y^{″} + 12y^{′} + 40y = 0$, where $y = y(x)$. If $y = e^{λx}$ is a solution, then $λ =$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186088241 1071 "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}+40y=0,$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088241 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+40{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +40) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +40=0\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +40 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36)-36+40 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +6 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-6\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda =-6\\pm 2\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086094 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186088245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335497 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088245 1071 "Given the equation $y^{″} - 14y^{′} + 49y = 0$ for $y = y(t)$, which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186088245 1071 "Given the equation ${y}^{″}-14{y}^{′}+49y=0$ for $y=y(t),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088245 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-14\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+49{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -7)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =7$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{7t}$ and $t{e}^{7t}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $7{e}^{t}+t{e}^{7t}$ is not a solution." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086244 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186088270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335420 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088270 1071 "Find the solution to the equation $e^{1 - 2x} = 5$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186088270 1071 "Find the solution to the equation ${e}^{1-2x}=5$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088270 1074 "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-2x} & =5 \\\\ ln⁡({e}^{1-2x}) & =ln⁡(5) \\\\ 1-2x & =ln⁡(5) \\\\ -2x & =ln⁡(5)-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1-ln⁡(5)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx≈-0.305\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086280 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186088276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335638 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088276 1071 "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-4,\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{t}{2},\\,-1\\le t\\le 5,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $a$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $5$ | $12$ | $21$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $b$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $c$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a-b+c.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088276 1071 "For the parametric curve\n$x = t^{2} - 4, y = \\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{t}{2},-1 \\le t \\le 5$,\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $a$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $5$ | $12$ | $21$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $b$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $c$ |\n\nFind the value of $a - b + c$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186088276 1074 "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-1$ into $x={t}^{2}-4.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=(-1)^{2}-4=1-4=-3.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=1$ into $y=\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{t}{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=5$ into $y=\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{t}{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na-b+c=-3-2+0=-5.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086292 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186088278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335622 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088278 1071 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186088278 1071 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$x = 3\\cos θ, y = 3\\sin θ, 0 \\le θ < 2π$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186088278 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =9(1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186092341 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335867 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186092341 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335868 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186092367 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335937 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186092368 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335938 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186092804 1068 #edb/ref 17592186090994 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186092804 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335608 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186092804 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186092804 1071 "What is the first term that is less than $0.0006$ in the following geometric sequence?\n$64, 32, 16, 8, …$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186092804 1071 "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,6$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n64,\\,32,\\,16,\\,8,\\,…\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186092804 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=64$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{32}{64}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={2}^{6}\\cdot {2}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={2}^{7-n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,6.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,6 \\\\ {2}^{7-n} & <0.000\\,6 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(2)}^{7-n} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6) \\\\ (7-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6) \\\\ 7-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}-7 \\\\ n & >7-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n7-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}≈17.703\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $18$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,6.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186092805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091000 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186092805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335269 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186092805 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186092805 1071 "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $108$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{3}{2}$. What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1984$?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186092805 1071 "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $108$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{3}{2}.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,984?$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186092805 1074 "Since we are given that ${a}_{4}=108$ and  $r=\\frac{3}{2}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4-1} \\\\ 108 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3} \\\\ 108 & =\\frac{27}{8}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,984.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >1\\,984 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >62 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(62) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(62) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1≈11.179\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,984.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186096612 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335853 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186096615 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335849 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186096615 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335852 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186096615 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335854 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186096689 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336006 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186096689 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336007 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186096689 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336012 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186096697 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335987 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186096800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335986 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186097331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095880 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186097331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335417 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186097331 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186097331 1071 "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(4+6x{)}^{3}.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186097331 1071 "Find $\\frac{df}{dx}$ when $f(x) = (4 + 6x)^{3}$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186097331 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{3},\\,g=4+6x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=3{g}^{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =3\\cdot 6\\cdot (4+6x{)}^{2} \\\\  & =18(4+6x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186097346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095970 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186097346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335447 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186097346 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186097346 1071 "![](images/f2/f25561a25abf75f301cbd29c6d6c0e2a0b61a389b757c06cd883819642f30545.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCEF$ shown above, which of the following are equal to the vector $BF^(→)$?\n\n1. $BC^(→) + CE^(→) + EF^(→)$\n2. $FA^(→) - BA^(→)$\n3. $BC^(→) + CF^(→)$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186097346 1071 "![](images/f2/f25561a25abf75f301cbd29c6d6c0e2a0b61a389b757c06cd883819642f30545.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCEF$ shown above, which of the following are equal to the vector $\\overset{\\to }{BF}?$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}+\\overset{\\to }{CE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{FA}-\\overset{\\to }{BA}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}+\\overset{\\to }{CF}$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186097346 1074 "- For the $1$st expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BC}+\\overset{\\to }{CE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF} & =(\\overset{\\to }{BC}+\\overset{\\to }{CE})+\\overset{\\to }{EF} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BF}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $2$nd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FA}-\\overset{\\to }{BA} & =\\overset{\\to }{FA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{FB} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{BF}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $3$rd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BC}+\\overset{\\to }{CF} & =\\overset{\\to }{BF}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186100848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335866 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186100885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336026 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186100885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336027 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186100885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336028 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186100885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336029 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186100885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336030 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186100885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336031 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186100895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335966 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186100895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335967 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186100985 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335948 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186100997 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335995 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186100998 1038 #ed