 true] [17592186115932 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0 \\le x < 2π$?\n\n1. $\\csc x = 3\\frac{1}{2}$\n2. $\\csc x = 1\\frac{1}{2}$\n3. $\\csc x = \\frac{1}{5}$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186115932 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=3$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186115932 1074 "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186115943 1068 #edb/ref 17592186114565 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186115943 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335330 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186115943 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186115943 1071 "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\ln (x - 3) + 1$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186115943 1071 "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)+1.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186115943 1074 "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x)$, we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=3.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186115944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186114571 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186115944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335627 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186115944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186115944 1071 "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x) =-\\log (2 - x) + 3$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186115944 1071 "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{log}⁡(2-x)+3.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186115944 1074 "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y=\\operatorname{log}⁡x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x),$ we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-x & =0 \\\\ -x & =-2 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186119745 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335715 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186119754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335736 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186119755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335737 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186119755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335738 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186119755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335740 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186119764 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335745 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186119764 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335746 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186119777 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335971 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186119790 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335976 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186119849 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336032 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186119954 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335748 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186119962 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335958 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186119962 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335960 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186119963 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335961 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186119963 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335962 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186120498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186119072 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186120498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335486 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186120498 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186120498 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t) = \\sin tp(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t = \\frac{π}{4}$, given that $p(\\frac{π}{4}) = i - j + 2k$ and $p^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) = i + j$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186120498 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,p(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, given that  $p(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\,i-\\,j+2k\\,$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\,i+\\,j.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186120498 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot p(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,p(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})p(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){p}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-\\,j+2k)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+\\,j) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(i+k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}(i+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n $T(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+k).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186120505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186119114 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186120505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335626 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186120505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186120505 1071 "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t) = ⟨e^{t}, e^{-t}⟩$ at $t = 0$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186120505 1071 "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ at $t=0.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186120505 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t},\\,y(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-(-{e}^{-t})\\cdot {e}^{t}|}{{(({e}^{t})^{2}+(-{e}^{-t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{2}{{({e}^{2(0)}+{e}^{-2(0)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{2}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186120510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186119144 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186120510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335528 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186120510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186120510 1074 "Notice that $C$ is a matrix for which all entries *below* the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nHence, it is an upper triangular matrix. \n\nBy contrast, matrices $A$, $B$, $D$, and $E$ each have at least one non-zero entry below the diagonal:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, they are not upper triangular matrices." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335774 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124445 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335775 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124446 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335788 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124446 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335790 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124448 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335793 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124448 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335794 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124453 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335806 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124455 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335807 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335808 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124469 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335810 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124472 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335828 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124478 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335892 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124480 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335891 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124480 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335899 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124480 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335905 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124480 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335907 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124480 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335909 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124480 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335910 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124480 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335912 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124482 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335768 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124568 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335778 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124568 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335779 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124568 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335783 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124569 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335776 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124569 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335777 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124668 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335949 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124668 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335950 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124668 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335951 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124670 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335952 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124670 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335953 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124670 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335954 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186124676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335847 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123680 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186125185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335389 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125185 1071 "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x, y) = \\ln (y - 2x^{2})$?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186125185 1071 "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2{x}^{2})?$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125185 1074 "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nNotice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2{x}^{2}) & =c \\\\ y-2{x}^{2} & ={e}^{c} \\\\ y & =2{x}^{2}+{e}^{c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of parabolas with the vertex at $(0,{e}^{c}).$ Therefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/65/6521da99a82e38a68a5aa87a0c7c88eacf4ed7b78351af594ec309fa2cb7f57b.png)" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123698 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186125188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335301 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125188 1071 "The function $y_{1}(x) = x^{3}$ is a solution to the equation $x^{2}y^{″} - xy^{′} - 3y = 0$ for $x > 0$. Use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_{2}(x)$ to the equation." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186125188 1071 "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}-3y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125188 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-x({u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2})-3u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-{u}^{′}x-3u-3u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x+5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}(-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-4$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123710 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186125190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335570 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125190 1071 "The function $y_{1}(x) = x$ is a solution to the equation $x^{2}y^{″} + 5xy^{′} - 5y = 0$ for $x > 0$. If $y_{2} = u(x)y_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)$?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186125190 1071 "The function ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}-5y=0$ for $x>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125190 1074 "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=ux.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=ux$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}x+u \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′})+5x({u}^{′}x+u)-5ux & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{3}+2{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+5{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+5ux-5ux & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{3}+7{u}^{′}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}x+7{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{2}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nx{u}^{″}+7{u}^{′}=0.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123728 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186125193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335401 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125193 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x, y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if\n$f_{x}(x, y) = ax^{2}y - 4y^{2} + 3, f_{y}(x, y) = 2x^{3} + bxy - 8$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186125193 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(x,y)=a{x}^{2}y-4{y}^{2}+3,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=2{x}^{3}+bxy-8.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125193 1074 "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(a{x}^{2}y-4{y}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =a{x}^{2}-8y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}+bxy-8) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+by\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{x}^{2}-8y=6{x}^{2}+by.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of ${x}^{2}$ and $y,$ respectively, we see that $a=6$ and $b=-8.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123764 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186125199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335371 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125199 1071 "Given that $f(x, y) = x\\ln (xy)$ and\n$\\frac{∂f}{∂x} = 1 + \\ln (xy), \\frac{∂f}{∂y} = \\frac{x}{y}$,\nfind the set of values $(x, y)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186125199 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125199 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ is defined only when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ is defined only when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123776 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186125201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335315 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125201 1071 "Given that $f(x) = x^{2} + 2$, on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n1. $x ∈ (-2, 2)$\n2. $x ∈ [0, ∞)$\n3. $x ∈ (- ∞, 0]$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186125201 1071 "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}+2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-2,2)$\n- $x\\in [0,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,0]$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125201 1074 "The graph of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units up to get $f(x)={x}^{2}+2.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff3d1ac7500d83574b8671b23e99d4a0f1dbd6ab9cebc906aa06ff2c21f95345.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,2).$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/a7/a71ce036adc5dc0b15fce9e367a0c8512699dc6a7814f4e9a1886696d2abad6e.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the intervals $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and  $x\\in (-\\infty ,0].$ On these intervals, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/68/6858bd1fab8d9659c84fa941cd63ee9f509c8364dce0e753706c640ecfe6e9ec.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123782 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186125202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335244 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125202 1071 "Given that $f(x) = x + 1$, on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n1. $x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$\n2. $x ∈ (-1, ∞)$\n3. $x ∈ (-10, 5)$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186125202 1071 "Given that $f(x)=x+1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-1,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-10,5)$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125202 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $1$ and $y$-intercept $1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/00/00d6394343d441ee10fa20ee029b8f89b52ff1e6ded8e7fdbe0756118212f1a8.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (-1,\\infty )$ and $x\\in (-10,5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123812 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186125207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335469 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125207 1071 "Calculate $lim_(x → π/3)\\csc (2x)$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186125207 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186125207 1074 "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186129105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335865 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186129130 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335869 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335695 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147435 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335696 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147436 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335697 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147437 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335698 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147438 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335699 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147438 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335700 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335749 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335750 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147440 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335752 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147440 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335764 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147441 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335765 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147442 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335751 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147442 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335760 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147442 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335761 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147442 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335762 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147442 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335763 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335719 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335720 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147445 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335721 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147446 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335722 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147447 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335716 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147448 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335717 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147449 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335718 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335723 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147451 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335731 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147451 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335732 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147452 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335724 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147452 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335725 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147454 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335726 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147455 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335727 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147455 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335728 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147455 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335729 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147455 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335730 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335733 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335734 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147506 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335694 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147514 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335707 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147514 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335708 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147515 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335712 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147519 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335711 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147520 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335709 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147520 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335710 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147533 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335713 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186147533 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335714 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146528 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335555 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148134 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148134 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148134 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 16y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148134 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ is given by:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\omega }\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146540 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335692 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148136 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148136 1071 "A particle of mass $12\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=24\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148136 1071 "A particle of mass $12kg$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 24kg/s^{2}$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148136 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{24}{12}=2.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=0.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146588 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335579 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148144 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $L{f^{″}(t)}$ for $s > 0$, given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous on $[0, ∞)$, $f^{′}(0) =-1$, $f(0) = 1$, and\n$L{f(t)} = \\frac{s - 1}{s^{2} + 1}, s > 0$.\nAssume that $f^{′}$ is continuous and $f^{″}$ piecewise continuous on $[0, ∞)$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148144 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $L{{f}^{″}(t)}$ for $s>0,$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous on $[0,\\infty ),$ ${f}^{′}(0)=-1,$ $f(0)=1,$ and\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{s-1}{{s}^{2}+1},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${f}^{′}$ is continuous and ${f}^{″}$ piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148144 1074 "Suppose $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ where $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[0,\\infty ).$ If ${f}^{′}(t)$ is continuous and ${f}^{″}(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{″}(t)} & ={s}^{2}L{f(t)}-sf(0)-{f}^{′}(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of ${f}^{″}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{″}(t)} & ={s}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{s-1}{{s}^{2}+1}-s\\cdot 1-(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}(s-1)}{{s}^{2}+1}-s+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}(s-1)}{{s}^{2}+1}-(s-1) \\\\  & =(s-1)(\\frac{{s}^{2}}{{s}^{2}+1}-1) \\\\  & =(s-1)(-\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-s}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146600 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335532 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148146 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $f^{′}(t)$ for $s > 3$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous over $[0, ∞)$, $f(0) = 2$, and\n$L{f(t)} = \\frac{2s - 1}{s^{2} - s - 6}, s > 3$.\nAssume that $f^{′}$ is piecewise continuous on $[0, ∞)$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148146 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of ${f}^{′}(t)$ for $s>3$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous over $[0,\\infty ),$ $f(0)=2,$ and\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{2s-1}{{s}^{2}-s-6},\\,s>3.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${f}^{′}$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148146 1074 "Suppose $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ where $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[0,\\infty ).$ If ${f}^{′}(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ),$ then\n\n$$\nL{{f}^{′}(t)}=sL{f(t)}-f(0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of ${f}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{′}(t)} & =sL{f(t)}-f(0) \\\\  & =s\\cdot \\frac{2s-1}{{s}^{2}-s-6}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{s(2s-1)-2({s}^{2}-s-6)}{{s}^{2}-s-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+12}{{s}^{2}-s-6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>3.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146654 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335339 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148155 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = L{y(t)}$ for $s \\ge \\sqrt{2}$ if\n\n$y^{″} - 2y = t^{2}, y(0) = 0, y^{′}(0) = 2$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148155 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ for $s\\ge \\sqrt{2}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2y={t}^{2},\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148155 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}-2y} & =L{{t}^{2}} \\\\ L{{y}^{″}}-2L{y} & =L{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{″}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a second derivative:\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{″}}={s}^{2}L{y}-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)\n$$\n\nTaking into account the given initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{″}}={s}^{2}Y(s)-2.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can solve for $Y(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}}-2L{y} & =L{{t}^{2}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}Y(s)-2)-2Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-2)Y(s) & =2+\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{2({s}^{3}+1)}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{2({s}^{3}+1)}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148160 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146684 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148160 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335484 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148160 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148160 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x = 0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in $x^{3}$, of the initial value problem\n$y^{″} = y + 2\\cos (x), y(0) =-1, y^{′}(0) = 0$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148160 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=y+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,y(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148160 1074 "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=-1,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =y(0)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =-1+2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}={y}^{′}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & ={y}^{′}(0)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =-0-0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+x\\cdot 0+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}\\cdot 0+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146696 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335436 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148162 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148162 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x = 0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in $x^{3}$, of the initial value problem\n$y^{′} = x + ye^{x}, y(0) = 1$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148162 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=1.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148162 1074 "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{′}(0),$  ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=1,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+y(0){e}^{0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{″}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=1+{y}^{′}{e}^{x}+y{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{″}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =1+{y}^{′}(0){e}^{0}+y(0){e}^{0} \\\\  & =1+1+1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate ${y}^{″}(x)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)={y}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{y}^{′}{e}^{x}+y{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & ={y}^{″}(0){e}^{0}+2{y}^{′}(0){e}^{0}+y(0){e}^{0} \\\\  & =3+2+1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =1+x\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot 3+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}\\cdot 6+⋯ \\\\  & =1+x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146708 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335643 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148164 1071 "Let $y = ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $y^{″} - y = 0$. Find $f(n)$, given that the recurrence relation for $a_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$a_{n + 2} = (a_{n})/(f(n)), n = 0, 1, 2…$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148164 1071 "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148164 1074 "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-{a}_{n}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=(n+1)(n+2).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146738 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335408 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148169 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148169 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148169 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing our equation with the above, we can see that the only equation that represents damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146744 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335590 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148170 1071 "Let $y = ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $4y^{′} + xy = 0$.\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients $a_{n}$, where $n = 1, 2, 3, …$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148170 1071 "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $4{y}^{′}+xy=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148170 1074 "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }4(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the second series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }4(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n4{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n4{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[4(n+1){a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,4(n+1){a}_{n+1}=-{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{4(n+1)},\\,n=1,2,3,….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146750 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335508 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148171 1071 "A particle of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c=4\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148171 1071 "A particle of mass $2kg$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 10kg/s^{2}$. The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c = 4kg/s$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148171 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}=-4{y}^{′}-10y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=0\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146756 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335603 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148172 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148172 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+10y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148172 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 10y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148172 1074 "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=3\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146762 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335566 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148173 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t > 0$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion with damping?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148173 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t>0.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion with damping?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148173 1074 "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+4y=\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146768 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335250 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148174 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148174 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=10{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)+{e}^{-2t}$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148174 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 9y = 10e^{-t}$.\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)$?\n\n1. $y = R\\cos (3t + ϕ) + e^{-t}$\n2. $y = R\\sin (t + ϕ) + e^{-2t}$\n3. $y = A\\cos 3t + B\\sin 3t + e^{-t}$\n\nNote that $A$, $B$, $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148174 1074 "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$\nIt can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha {e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$\n\nOnly I and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148175 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146774 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148175 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335534 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148175 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148175 1071 "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=15\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ \nAn external force $F(t)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148175 1071 "A particle of mass $3kg$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 15kg/s^{2}$.\nAn external force $F(t) = 9\\sin (t)N$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148175 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-15y+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146810 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335472 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148181 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148181 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+25y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+3y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148181 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 2y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n2. $y^{″} + 10y^{′} + 25y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n3. $y^{″} + 5y^{′} + 3y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148181 1074 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={2}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =-4<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+25y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={10}^{2}-4(1)(25) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+3y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +3=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(3) & =13>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146822 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335372 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148183 1071 "A particle of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's speed, and has a damping coefficient $c=10\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148183 1071 "A particle of mass $2kg$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 18kg/s^{2}$. The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's speed, and has a damping coefficient $c = 10kg/s$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148183 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}=-10{y}^{′}-18y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+9y=0\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146834 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335538 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148185 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148185 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{″} + 3y^{′} + 7y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n2. $y^{″} + 6y^{′} + 8y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping.\n3. $y^{″} + 8y^{′} + 16y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148185 1074 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +7=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(7) & =-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(8) & =4>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement II is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(16) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146846 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335266 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148187 1071 "The position $y(t)$, measured in inches, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion with light damping is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 5y = 34\\cos 2t$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $π$ seconds." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148187 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in inches, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion with light damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=34\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ seconds." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148187 1074 "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion with light damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t).\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is $34\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2,$ $\\beta =8.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=-2,\\,Q=-9.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-{e}^{-t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-{e}^{-\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )) \\\\  & =2(1-{e}^{-\\pi })\\,\\text{in}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146852 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335547 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148188 1074 "The second-order Cauchy–Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}y′′+bxy′+cy=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that has the above form is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+4y=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146870 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335302 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148191 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186148191 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$x^{2}y^{″} - xy^{′} + y = 0, x > 0$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186148191 1074 "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +1] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lamb