itute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6\\alpha x)-2x(3\\alpha {x}^{2})+2(\\alpha {x}^{3}) & =2{x}^{3} \\\\ (6\\alpha -6\\alpha +2\\alpha ){x}^{3} & =2{x}^{3} \\\\ 2\\alpha {x}^{3} & =2{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get $\\alpha =1.$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150239 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186152627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335326 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152627 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152627 1071 "Find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ of the equation\n$x^{2}\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 3x\\frac{dy}{dx} + 4y = 4\\ln x, x > 0$.\n\n*Hint: Consider using the substitution $x = e^{t}$.*" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186152627 1071 "Find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Consider using the substitution $x={e}^{t}.$*" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152627 1074 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+4y=4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{2t}+Bt{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-4\\alpha +4(\\alpha t+\\beta )=4t\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the