{6}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152635 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150287 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186152635 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335588 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152635 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152635 1071 "For the differential equation $x^{2}y^{″} + xy^{′} + y = x^{3}$, what is the associated homogeneous equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186152635 1071 "For the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y={x}^{3},$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152635 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150305 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186152638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335605 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152638 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152638 1071 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=10$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′}+16y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=0$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152638 1071 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n1. $xy^{″} - 2y^{′} + y = x^{2} + \\sin x, y(1) = 0, y^{′}(1) = 1$\n2. $x^{2}y^{″} - xy^{′} + y = 10$\n3. $x^{2}y^{″} - 4xy^{′} + 16y = 0, y(0) = 0, y^{′}(2) = 0$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186152638 1074 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150311 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186152639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335304 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152639 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152639 1071 "For the differential equation $x^{2}y^{″} - y^{′} + 3y = e^{x}$, what is the associated homogeneous equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186152639 1071 "For the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+3y={e}^{x},$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152639 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+3y=0.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150317 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186152640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335340 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152640 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152640 1071 "Given that $y_{1}(x) = e^{x}$ and $y_{2}(x) = e^{2x}$ are solutions to the equation $y^{″} - 3y^{′} + 2y = 0$, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n1. $y(x) = e^{x} + e^{2x}$\n2. $y(x) = e^{x} \\cdot e^{2x}$\n3. $y(x) = \\frac{e^{x}}{e^{2x}}$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186152640 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152640 1074 "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150341 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186152644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335630 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152644 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152644 1071 "Given that $y_{1}(t) = t$ and $y_{2}(t) = t^{2}$ are solutions to the equation $t^{2}y^{″} - 2ty^{′} + 2y = 0$ for $t > 0$, what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186152644 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)=t$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}$ are solutions to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-2t{y}^{′}+2y=0$ for $t>0,$ what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152644 1074 "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)=t$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{t-{t}^{2}}{2}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150347 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186152645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335506 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152645 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152645 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x,\n$$\n\nsolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-4.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152645 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$y^{″} + 16y = 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y = A\\sin 4x + B\\cos 4x$,\n\nsolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$y^{″} + 16y = 0, y(0) = 1, y^{′}(0) =-4$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186152645 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-4,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-4B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=-4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4=4A\\,⟹\\,A=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152696 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150653 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186152696 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335264 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152696 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152696 1071 "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186152696 1071 "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n$x = 2\\cos θ, y = \\sin θ, 0 \\le θ < 2π$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186152696 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186171962 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336000 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186171963 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336001 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186171963 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336002 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186171963 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336003 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186171963 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336004 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186172599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171189 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186172599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335348 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186172599 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186172599 1071 "Use Pascal's triangle to determine the value of $(\\frac{5}{4})$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186172599 1071 "Use Pascal's triangle to determine the value of $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4}).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186172599 1074 "To find the value of $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $5$ and position $4$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe value located in row $5$ and position $4$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})=5.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186172600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171195 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186172600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335354 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186172600 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186172600 1071 "Use Pascal's triangle to determine the value of $(\\frac{3}{1})$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186172600 1071 "Use Pascal's triangle to determine the value of $(\\frac{3}{1}).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186172600 1074 "To find the value of $(\\frac{3}{1}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $3$ and position $1$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe value located in row $3$ and position $1$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(\\frac{3}{1})=3.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186172602 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171207 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186172602 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335316 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186172602 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186172602 1071 "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3}{1})=[]+(\\frac{2}{1})\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186172602 1071 "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n$(\\frac{3}{1}) = [(\\frac{2}{0})] + (\\frac{2}{1})$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186172602 1074 "To find $(\\frac{3}{1}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $3$ and position $1$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $(\\frac{3}{1})$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{1}{0}) &  & (\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{2}{0}) &  & (\\frac{2}{1}) &  & (\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  & (\\frac{3}{0}) &  & (\\frac{3}{1}) &  & (\\frac{3}{2}) &  & (\\frac{3}{3}) \\\\  &  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(\\frac{3}{1})$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3}{1})=(\\frac{2}{0})+(\\frac{2}{1})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(\\frac{2}{0}).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186176056 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335894 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186176057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335833 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186176058 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335895 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186176058 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335896 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186176059 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335830 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186176059 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335831 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186176060 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335897 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186176060 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335898 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186176062 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335901 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186176062 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335903 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186179655 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336023 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186179794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335914 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186179794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335921 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186179794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335922 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186179794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335923 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186184155 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335851 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186184831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183565 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186184831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335662 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186184831 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186184831 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\cos x =-\\frac{4}{5}, \\sin y = \\frac{12}{13}, \\cos y = \\frac{5}{13}$.\n\nGiven that $x$ lies in the second quadrant, find the value of $\\sin (y - x)$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186184831 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\frac{12}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=\\frac{5}{13}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $x$ lies in the second quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186184831 1074 "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡x=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡x & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(-4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(\\frac{12}{13})(-\\frac{4}{5})-(\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{65}-\\frac{15}{65} \\\\  & =-\\frac{63}{65}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186184835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183589 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186184835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335261 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186184835 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186184835 1071 "![](images/25/25cdbb7ea69245986f5fc3df65490aa6ca72d0adcf319a768c5243d7b692e3d8.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = \\cot (Bx) + D$, where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B - 2D$?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186184835 1071 "![](images/25/25cdbb7ea69245986f5fc3df65490aa6ca72d0adcf319a768c5243d7b692e3d8.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B-2D?$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186184835 1074 "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{5})}=5.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{3}{2}$ units up. Hence, $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $B-2D=5-2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})=5-3=2.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x)+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186184836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183595 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186184836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335402 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186184836 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186184836 1071 "![](images/4a/4afd6e9f2b3343a8364f088653aa99cc4ceeeeef7c53f9014b2dd83314f66118.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = \\tan (\\frac{x}{2} + C)$, where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{π}{2} < C < \\frac{π}{2}$, what is the value of $C$?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186184836 1071 "![](images/4a/4afd6e9f2b3343a8364f088653aa99cc4ceeeeef7c53f9014b2dd83314f66118.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186184836 1074 "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+2C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=tan\\,(\\frac{x}{2}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/8a/8a19beac86998c845d7ffa225a52d425ab48b9ec350f6c8fe42f7c6dbce027ce.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n2C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186188223 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335836 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186188223 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335837 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186188227 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335838 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186188228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335835 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186188228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335839 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186188976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186187902 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186188976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335274 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186188976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186188976 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186188976 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{1}{\\sec x - \\tan x}$." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186188976 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}=\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186192790 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335767 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186198362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336024 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186203969 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335871 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186204623 1068 #edb/ref 17592186203596 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186204623 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335369 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186204623 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186204623 1071 "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y = 3\\sin (2x + \\frac{π}{2})$? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186204623 1071 "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186204623 1074 "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=2x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =n\\pi  \\\\ 2x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is any integer." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186229145 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335766 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186229145 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335785 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186229754 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228704 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186229754 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335456 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186229754 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186229754 1071 "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L(y_{1}) = 4x$ and $L(y_{2}) = x^{2} + 1$ for some functions $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$. What is $L(y_{1} - 2y_{2})$?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186229754 1071 "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=4x$ and $L({y}_{2})={x}^{2}+1$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L({y}_{1}-2{y}_{2})?$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186229754 1074 "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L({y}_{1}-2{y}_{2}) & =L({y}_{1})-2L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =4x-2({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{2}+4x-2 \\\\  & =-2({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =-2(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186231433 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335735 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186231786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186231248 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186231786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335338 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186231786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186231786 1071 "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 16y = 2\\cos^{2} pt$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186231786 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=2{cos}^{2}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186231786 1074 "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =4.$ Since \n\n$$\nf(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡pt=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\frac{\\omega }{2}=2.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186234184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335245 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186234184 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186234184 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186234184 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 false] [17592186234184 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sq