13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335692 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334648 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335692 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334917 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335692 1075 #edb/ref 17592186335131 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335692 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335692 1077 "selection" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335692 1079 #edb/ref 17592186333817 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335692 1080 #uuid "feae3d7e-c538-54a0-818b-206dfc9ee4db" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335693 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333220 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335693 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333925 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335693 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334237 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335693 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334998 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335693 1075 #edb/ref 17592186335054 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335693 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335693 1077 "selection" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335693 1079 #edb/ref 17592186333220 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335693 1080 #uuid "ff6567b8-7965-5eba-baf1-a968a40eeaa8" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335694 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335666 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335694 1069 "q-64219" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335694 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335694 1071 "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n(4x+3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+(6xy-{x}^{3})\\text{d}y=0.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335694 1072 #uuid "7d8cab43-4389-52c8-83a2-bbac13e68994" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335694 1074 "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=4x+3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=6xy-{x}^{3}.$\n\nLet's check if the equation is exact. To do this, we check to see if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y)=6y-3{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy-{x}^{3})=6y-3{x}^{2}=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the given equation is exact and its solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (4x+3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}y+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (6xy-{x}^{3})\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}y+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=2{x}^{2},\\,f(y)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}y=c.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335494 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335695 1069 "q-64247" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335695 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335695 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335695 1072 #uuid "a7eb0e1d-b24c-55ed-8eda-b9aad34b46ce" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335695 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents SHM is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nIn the above case:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335696 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335670 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335696 1069 "q-64250" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335696 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335696 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335696 1072 #uuid "99cafc28-f3f8-515b-921f-bce059450c08" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335696 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=1\\,⟹\\,\\omega =1.\n$$\n\nThe period $T$ is given by:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\omega }\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{1}=2\\pi \\,\\text{s}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335697 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335620 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335697 1069 "q-64252" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335697 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335697 1071 "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+81y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335697 1072 #uuid "347ed2fe-a388-536f-85e7-6e55844b3b57" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335697 1074 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=81\\,⟹\\,\\omega =9.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335698 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335625 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335698 1069 "q-64253" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335698 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335698 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+64y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335698 1072 #uuid "525991ab-a7d5-5a16-ae31-a0c0ee48b9e7" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335698 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=64\\,⟹\\,\\omega =8.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8R\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4=8R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335699 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335474 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335699 1069 "q-64255" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335699 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335699 1071 "A particle of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=32\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335699 1072 #uuid "00bb2026-9dc7-5b3d-89c1-7d7f1373f0dc" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335699 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{32}{8}=4.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335700 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335380 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335700 1069 "q-64258" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335700 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335700 1071 "A particle of mass $32\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{4}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{4}$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335700 1072 #uuid "260eecb9-d0fb-5697-b47b-f5982b239650" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335700 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{8}{32}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}$ in form 1 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335701 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335267 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335701 1069 "q-64272" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335701 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335701 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335701 1072 #uuid "e646e3bf-d80d-58f2-b084-f7d1465b7f9a" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335701 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ So we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -2\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =0$ and $\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335702 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335526 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335702 1069 "q-64276" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335702 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335702 1071 "Find the general solution to the problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335702 1072 #uuid "dbdef734-fa80-5bda-a3b6-8a4413098790" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335702 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\pm \\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\ny_p = x (\\alpha \\cos x + \\beta \\sin x),\n]\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & =2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -2\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-4$ and $\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335336 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335703 1069 "q-64278" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335703 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335703 1071 "Find the general solutionto the problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335703 1072 #uuid "513613be-c972-585c-b378-0ab67f8a6280" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335703 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\pm 4\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+4x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & =8(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+16x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n8(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+16x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+16x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\,=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-8\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+8\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8\\beta =16\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x) \\\\ -8\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1$ and $\\beta =2.$ Therefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)=-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335704 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335289 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335704 1069 "q-64289" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335704 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335704 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-9\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+18y=8{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{6x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335704 1072 #uuid "4787725b-a39c-5e34-bfda-c3397e9c29ce" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335704 1074 "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{6x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =3$ and $\\lambda =6.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8{e}^{2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $2$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{2x}-9(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})+18(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =8{e}^{2x} \\\\ (4-18+18)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =8{e}^{2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =8{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335705 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335550 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335705 1069 "q-64292" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335705 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335705 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=2{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335705 1072 #uuid "c3c44946-3e1f-5512-b0db-6f8fdfa92179" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335705 1074 "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-4x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =-4.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2{e}^{-4x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-4$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (16x-8){e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (16x-8){e}^{-4x}+6(\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x})+8(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}) & =2{e}^{-4x} \\\\ (16x-8+6-24x+8x)\\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =2{e}^{-4x} \\\\ -2\\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =2{e}^{-4x} \\\\ -2\\alpha  & =2 \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x{e}^{-4x}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335706 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335286 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335706 1069 "q-64294" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335706 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335706 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=3{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335706 1072 #uuid "78a86fc9-6a91-5d47-ad39-d489551d9bef" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335706 1074 "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x},$ we have that $\\lambda =2$ is the double root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $3{e}^{2x}.$  In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $2$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (2{x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (4{x}^{2}+8x+2){e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (4{x}^{2}+8x+2){e}^{2x}-4(\\alpha (2{x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x})+4(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{2x}) & =3{e}^{2x} \\\\ (4{x}^{2}+8x+2-8{x}^{2}-8x+4{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =3 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335546 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335707 1069 "q-64324" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335707 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335707 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of ${f}^{′}(t)$ for $s>3$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous over $[0,\\infty ),$ $f(0)=2,$ and\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{2s-3}{{s}^{2}-3s},\\,s>3.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${f}^{′}$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335707 1072 #uuid "4b69a04a-07d1-5d7a-854a-f5080143650e" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335707 1074 "Suppose $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ where $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[0,\\infty ).$ If ${f}^{′}(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ),$ then\n\n$$\nL{{f}^{′}(t)}=sL{f(t)}-f(0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of ${f}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{′}(t)} & =sL{f(t)}-f(0) \\\\  & =s\\cdot \\frac{2s-3}{{s}^{2}-3s}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{s(2s-3)-2({s}^{2}-3s)}{{s}^{2}-3s} \\\\  & =\\frac{3s}{s(s-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>3.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335285 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335708 1069 "q-64325" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335708 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335708 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of ${f}^{′}(t)$ for $s>4$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous over $[0,\\infty ),$ $f(0)=1,$ and\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{s-4}{{s}^{2}-8s+20},\\,s>4.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${f}^{′}$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335708 1072 #uuid "1b827f53-7419-5c62-bee0-6a51ebcc21ce" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335708 1074 "Suppose $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ where $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[0,\\infty ).$ If ${f}^{′}(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ),$ then\n\n$$\nL{{f}^{′}(t)}=sL{f(t)}-f(0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of ${f}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{′}(t)} & =sL{f(t)}-f(0) \\\\  & =s\\cdot \\frac{s-4}{{s}^{2}-8s+20}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{s(s-4)-({s}^{2}-8s+20)}{{s}^{2}-8s+20} \\\\  & =\\frac{4s-20}{{s}^{2}-8s+20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>4.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335709 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335502 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335709 1069 "q-64348" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335709 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335709 1071 "${L}^{-1}{\\frac{4}{s-2}}=$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335709 1072 #uuid "175b4da0-72f2-53c5-ae4f-b0b073614e2d" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335709 1074 "From the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,s>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by applying the inverse Laplace transform to the above result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{L{{e}^{2t}}} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-2}} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-2}} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-2}} & ={e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the multiplicative property of the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-2}}=4\\cdot {e}^{2t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{4}{s-2}}=4{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335710 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335678 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335710 1069 "q-64353" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335710 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335710 1071 "Find the inverse Laplace transform for $F(s)=\\frac{8{e}^{-2s}}{s}.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335710 1072 #uuid "d122843b-fd89-53cf-afaa-95d930a92627" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335710 1074 "From the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{u}_{2}(t)} & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s},\\,s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nTherefore, by applying the inverse Laplace transform to the above result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{u}_{2}(t)} & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{L{{u}_{2}(t)}} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}} \\\\ {u}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}} & ={u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the multiplicative property of the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot {L}^{-1}{\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}}=8\\cdot {u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{8{e}^{-2s}}{s}}=8{u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335429 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335711 1069 "q-64359" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335711 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335711 1071 "Find ${L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ for $F(s)=\\frac{10+3{s}^{2}}{{s}^{3}}.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335711 1072 #uuid "f8a3733e-bc99-5017-9533-ebac37b0e2d6" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335711 1074 "We decompose the fraction into a sum of partial fractions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10+3{s}^{2}}{{s}^{3}}=\\frac{10}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{s}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0, \\\\ L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition and multiplication properties of the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{10}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{s}} & =5{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}}+3{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\  & =5{t}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{10+3{s}^{2}}{{s}^{3}}$ is $f(t)=5{t}^{2}+3.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335465 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335712 1069 "q-64408" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335712 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335712 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $L{{f}^{″}(t)}$ for $s>1,$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous on $[0,\\infty ),$ ${f}^{′}(0)=-1,$ $f(0)=2,$ and\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{2s+1}{{s}^{2}+s-2},\\,s>1.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${f}^{′}$ is continuous and ${f}^{″}$ piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335712 1072 #uuid "d1c5f5b2-4486-59c9-9cb4-c0f9d4deae05" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335712 1074 "Suppose $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ where $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[0,\\infty ).$ If ${f}^{′}(t)$ is continuous and ${f}^{″}(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{″}(t)} & ={s}^{2}L{f(t)}-sf(0)-{f}^{′}(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of ${f}^{″}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{″}(t)} & ={s}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2s+1}{{s}^{2}+s-2}-s\\cdot 2-(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}(2s+1)}{{s}^{2}+s-2}-(2s-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}(2s+1)-({s}^{2}+s-2)(2s-1)}{{s}^{2}+s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}(2s+1)-{s}^{2}(2s-1)-(s-2)(2s-1)}{{s}^{2}+s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}-(2{s}^{2}-5s+2)}{{s}^{2}+s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5s-2}{{s}^{2}+s-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>1.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335548 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335713 1069 "q-64480" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335713 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ for $s>0$ if\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=2+t,\\,y(0)=3.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335713 1072 #uuid "d38d9b82-be1d-50f0-9441-4296d5b91f2d" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335713 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+4y} & =L{2+t} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+4L{y} & =2L{1}+L{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=3:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-3\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},\\,s>0,\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+4L{y} & =2L{1}+L{t} \\\\ (sY(s)-3)+4Y(s) & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ sY(s)+4Y(s) & =3+\\frac{2}{s}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{3{s}^{2}+2s+1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{3{s}^{2}+2s+1}{{s}^{2}(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335714 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335577 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335714 1069 "q-64482" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335714 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335714 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ for $s>0$ if\n\n$$\n2{y}^{′}+2y=4+{t}^{2},\\,y(0)=0.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335714 1072 #uuid "6fc6fee8-a991-5b7c-a87e-e2f17af9e5e5" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335714 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{2{y}^{′}+2y} & =L{4+{t}^{2}} \\\\ 2L{{y}^{′}}+2L{y} & =4L{1}+L{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},\\,s>0,\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2L{{y}^{′}}+2L{y} & =4L{1}+L{{t}^{2}} \\\\ 2sY(s)+2Y(s) & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ (2s+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{4{s}^{2}+2}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{2(2{s}^{2}+1)}{{s}^{3}(2s+2)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}+1}{{s}^{3}(s+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335715 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335406 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335715 1069 "q-64492" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335715 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335715 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)={t}^{2}p(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t=1$, given that  $p(1)=i-\\,j-k\\,$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(1)=-i+\\,j+\\,k.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335715 1072 #uuid "b87595fa-e030-5e39-b17d-c9692165b1cf" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335715 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\cdot p(t)+{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =2t\\,p(t)+{t}^{2}\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =2(1)\\,p(1)+(1)^{2}\\,{p}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =2(i-\\,j-k)+(-i+\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =2i-2\\,j-2k-i+j+\\,k \\\\  & =i-j-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (i-j-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=1$ is  $T(1)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335716 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335594 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335716 1069 "q-64512" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335716 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335716 1071 "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $3{y}^{′}-y=0.$ \n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=0,1,2,…?$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335716 1072 #uuid "4900c366-989b-534e-b76c-e0ccf096d471" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335716 1074 "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[3(n+1){a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n3(n+1){a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)},\\,n=0,1,2,….\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335536 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335717 1069 "q-64531" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335717 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335717 1071 "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $3{y}^{″}+y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2,…\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335717 1072 #uuid "fd1af627-3522-5f37-95a0-fd810519ec94" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335717 1074 "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[3(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}+{a}_{n}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n3(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}+{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2,…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=-3(n+1)(n+2).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335718 1069 "q-64536" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335718 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335718 1071 "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation ${y}^{′}+7xy=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335718 1072 #uuid "47b13b13-633e-5a14-a957-f5bcb1c4ba43" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335718 1074 "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+7x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+7{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+7{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the second series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+7{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+7{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+7{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}+7{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,(n+1){a}_{n+1}=-7{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{7{a}_{n-1}}{n+1},\\,n=1,2,3,….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335248 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335719 1069 "q-64558" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335719 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335719 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=y-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335719 1072 #uuid "2e148412-3806-508b-9385-f01a76aebe80" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335719 1074 "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=-2,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =y(0)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =-2-2(0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}={y}^{′}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & ={y}^{′}(0)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-2(1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinaly, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\ y(x) & =-2+x\\cdot 2+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}\\cdot (-2)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}\\cdot 0+⋯ \\\\ y(x) & =-2+2x-{x}^{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335551 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335720 1069 "q-64560" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335720 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335720 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=y+4x+{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-4.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335720 1072 #uuid "b07970ab-3a9f-5b1f-bcb6-a718478d352a" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335720 1074 "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=1,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =y(0)+4(0)+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1+0+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}={y}^{′}+4+{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & ={y}^{′}(0)+4+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =-4+4+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =1+x\\cdot (-4)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}\\cdot 2+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}\\cdot 1+⋯ \\\\  & =1-4x+{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335721 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335495 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335721 1069 "q-64563" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335721 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335721 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=-1$, in ascending powers of $(x+1)$ up to and including the term in $(x+1)^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}={x}^{2}y-{y}^{′},\\,y(-1)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=0.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335721 1072 #uuid "36e78bb3-1c0f-5d66-b4f1-bf8667a27b77" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335721 1074 "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=-1$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(-1)+(x+1){y}^{′}(-1)+\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(-1)+\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(-1)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(-1)$ and ${y}^{′}(-1),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(-1)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(-1).$\n\nWe substitute $y(-1)=3$ and ${y}^{′}(-1)=0$ into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(-1) & =(-1)^{2}y(-1)-{y}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-0 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)=2xy+{x}^{2}{y}^{′}-{y}^{″}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(-1)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(-1) & =2(-1)y(-1)+(-1)^{2}{y}^{′}(-1)-{y}^{″}(-1) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3)+((-1)^{2}\\cdot 0)-3 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(-1)+(x+1){y}^{′}(-1)+\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(-1)+\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(-1)+⋯ \\\\  & =3+(x+1)\\cdot 0+\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}\\cdot 3+\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{6}\\cdot (-9)+… \\\\  & =3+\\frac{3}{2}(x+1)^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}(x+1)^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335722 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335722 1069 "q-64565" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335722 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335722 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-y{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=-1.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335722 1072 #uuid "4675f8c9-0b87-542f-a400-7af62b545a82" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335722 1074 "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{′}(0),$  ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=-1,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =(0)^{2}-y(0){e}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{″}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=2x-{y}^{′}{e}^{x}-y{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{″}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =2(0)-{y}^{′}(0){e}^{0}-y(0){e}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(1\\cdot 1)-(-1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate ${y}^{″}(x)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(x) & =2-{y}^{″}{e}^{x}-2{y}^{′}{e}^{x}-y{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & =2-{y}^{″}(0){e}^{0}-2{y}^{′}(0){e}^{0}-y(0){e}^{0} \\\\  & =2-(0\\cdot 1)-(2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1)-((-1)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =2-0-2+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+x\\cdot (1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}\\cdot 1+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335618 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335723 1069 "q-64588" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335723 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335723 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335723 1072 #uuid "cae0aa12-cdd6-590e-99eb-66b4c8176aa4" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335723 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing our equation with the above, we can see that the only equation that represents damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}+6y=0.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335724 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335446 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335724 1069 "q-64591" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335724 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335724 1071 "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=15\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c=9\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335724 1072 #uuid "5f786c29-e425-58af-9063-5fe1b456dd96" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335724 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-9{y}^{′}-15y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+5y=0\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335725 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335488 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335725 1069 "q-64592" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335725 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335725 1071 "A particle of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's speed, and has a damping coefficient $c=6\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335725 1072 #uuid "d1e89040-067b-5e6c-a0cb-d3d762298784" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335725 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n4{y}^{″}=-6{y}^{′}-8y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+1.5{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335726 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335387 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335726 1069 "q-64596" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335726 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335726 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t>0.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion without damping?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335726 1072 #uuid "1617e3b1-0c82-5e4f-9ee5-ab5a7b9879bb" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335726 1074 "The general equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=4{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335454 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335727 1069 "q-64597" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335727 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335727 1071 "A particle of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=12\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ \nAn external force $F(t)=10{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335727 1072 #uuid "c5b05caa-b039-5e33-b724-43c1b816b031" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335727 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}=-12y+10{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6y=5{e}^{2t}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335324 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335728 1069 "q-64599" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335728 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335728 1071 "A particle of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ \nAn external force $F(t)=12{t}^{3}\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335728 1072 #uuid "07b06f33-b4ee-5f40-b29c-4546e0a946ba" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335728 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n4{y}^{″}=-18y+12{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4.5y=3{t}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335729 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335365 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335729 1069 "q-64600" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335729 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335729 1071 "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=12\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c=9\\,\\text{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335729 1072 #uuid "23f2ae10-1325-5e77-a3d0-434c6abe321a" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335729 1074 "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-9{y}^{′}-12y+15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+4y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335373 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335730 1069 "q-64602" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335730 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335730 1071 "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=15\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c=12\\,\\text{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=6{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335730 1072 #uuid "b73f9c66-9788-57ff-804f-903c7ca148d5" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335730 1074 "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-12{y}^{′}-15y+6{e}^{4t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+5y=2{e}^{4t}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335731 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335321 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335731 1069 "q-64615" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335731 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335731 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335731 1072 #uuid "0dca080f-586e-56e2-9297-7dcd4b704b21" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335731 1074 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(8) & =-16<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +2=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335732 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335559 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335732 1069 "q-64616" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335732 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335732 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335732 1072 #uuid "7c1c35ed-7049-53ec-934c-7b92d30ad741" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335732 1074 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(6) & =1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={2}^{2}-4(1)(1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +2=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =8>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335733 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335645 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335733 1069 "q-64643" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335733 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335733 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $3\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}$, find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335733 1072 #uuid "3b5683d4-f207-5c86-9f78-566919581fe8" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335733 1074 "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is $12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.$ The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ because this is already included in the complimentary function. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is  \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=t(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.  \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =0,\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+(B+2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $3\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}$, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=0,\\,B=1.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=(1+2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =(1+2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335734 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335363 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335734 1069 "q-64645" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335734 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335734 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion with light damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+17y=40{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ second." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335734 1072 #uuid "5ebd03a0-9d4e-5010-aa14-fd85893d7a8e" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335734 1074 "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion with light damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+17y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +17 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t).\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is an exponential force. The constant factor in the exponent is $-2$, which is not a root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular exponential solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)+2{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=-2,\\,Q=1.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)+2{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & ={e}^{-\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4(\\pi )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4(\\pi ))+2{e}^{-3\\pi } \\\\  & =2{e}^{-\\pi }({e}^{-2\\pi }-1)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335735 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335580 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335735 1069 "q-64647" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335735 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335735 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335735 1072 #uuid "3cf0fed1-cc47-5050-81b5-0e3536d5ca7e" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335735 1074 "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =3.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=3.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335736 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335257 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335736 1069 "q-64656" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335736 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335736 1071 "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=\\pi $ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335736 1072 #uuid "bccec19e-0bd3-54c0-b2e0-223abfefcf81" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335736 1074 "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature: \n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=\\pi $:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{3})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,0⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\text{ }\\frac{1}{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335265 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335737 1069 "q-64661" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335737 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335737 1071 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+2\\,j-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,k,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335737 1072 #uuid "3e18d5a8-08c4-5591-8e9a-7f22a6b8acbe" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335737 1074 "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2({cos}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡2t+{cos}^{2}⁡2t)+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335523 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335738 1069 "q-64666" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335738 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335738 1071 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335738 1072 #uuid "fe9f4359-a5d3-51ab-bfaf-67dd4916cffb" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335738 1074 "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2})}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335414 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335739 1069 "q-64675" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335739 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335739 1071 "The angle $\\theta =-{395}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335739 1072 #uuid "d5324a1f-6f34-52eb-9030-0465e5ae23da" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335739 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{395}^{∘}$ by *adding* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- $-{395}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}=-{35}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{395}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={325}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${325}^{∘}$ is coterminal with $-{395}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/93/936e222cebb005bc504867f26eb2144bb976cab2441e53051121e056be158be1.png)\n\nWe see that ${325}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-{395}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335740 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335314 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335740 1069 "q-64680" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335740 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335740 1071 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)=⟨\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,t⟩.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335740 1072 #uuid "b6d968e1-179a-5b9e-896d-829a90d9d8fc" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335740 1074 "Let $T(t)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the parameter $t.$ Since we are given the curve $r(t)$ parametrized by the parameter $t$ (not the arc length $s$), we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)}^{2}+{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(3t))+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the corresponding unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\sqrt{(9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}+(-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\sqrt{{9}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡3t+{cos}^{2}⁡3t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\,\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\,}{\\sqrt{10}}=\\frac{9}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335581 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335741 1069 "q-64681" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335741 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335741 1071 "![](images/94/94b8650466747f6858b30adbb8962e6c91159466b570f536d4999e2b08c31b67.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{2},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335741 1072 #uuid "e9c7086f-08af-5d07-8de2-8cc3da9eb077" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335741 1074 "![](images/cb/cb569d4b7b76699259d2167b8c930e636e3fb794b14ef25a4d909c224235d7c3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335742 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335273 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335742 1069 "q-64698" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335742 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335742 1071 "Given that $y=y(t)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+64y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335742 1072 #uuid "9b13d315-60ad-5f09-add0-7ddb741771b7" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335742 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+64{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+64) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+64=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the auxiliary equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-64 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 8\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lambda =\\pm 8\\text{i}$ are the (purely imaginary) roots of the auxiliary equation. \n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{8\\text{i}t})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{8\\text{i}t})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t$ is not a solution." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335743 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335438 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335743 1069 "q-64706" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335743 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335743 1071 "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-3y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335743 1072 #uuid "29ee4fe8-3de9-5ffb-adea-6b9fd8781620" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335743 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-3{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-1$ or $\\lambda =3.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-x}$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $-5{e}^{-3x}$ is not a solution." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335744 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335325 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335744 1069 "q-64730" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335744 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335744 1071 "![](images/2f/2f01b946a5c7c0f93c2171379603c4426913af5a15d0bc03434785144cfe4102.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ what is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335744 1072 #uuid "8585ed26-d09c-5c8e-84bf-c2d2c03840ec" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335744 1074 "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nUsing the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we finally get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=1.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335745 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335533 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335745 1069 "q-64732" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335745 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335745 1071 "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\,k\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335745 1072 #uuid "a6d0a779-de60-5fed-8e29-8de76943c6c3" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335745 1074 "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature: \n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})k \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j] \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{9})}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335746 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335294 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335746 1069 "q-64738" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335746 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335746 1071 "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335746 1072 #uuid "85d6e3bb-1239-5725-994c-6f104c6ddc40" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335746 1074 "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature: \n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,-\\frac{4}{5}(-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{8}{25}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}))}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{8}{25})}=\\frac{25}{8}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335747 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335481 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335747 1069 "q-64747" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335747 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335747 1071 "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335747 1072 #uuid "e41bd3a2-77cd-50f9-af0d-7902e2113a96" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335747 1074 "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =({e}^{x}){(x{e}^{x})}^{′}-(x{e}^{x}){({e}^{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})-(x{e}^{x})({e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}-x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335748 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335673 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335748 1069 "q-64761" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335748 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335748 1071 "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}},\\,{e}^{t}⟩$ at the point where $t=0.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335748 1072 #uuid "71c558ef-3d89-5d4c-b532-d4626f624683" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335748 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}},\\,{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}},\\,{e}^{0}⟩=⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{(1-{t}^{2})}^{3/2}},\\,{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{(1-{0}^{2})^{3/2}},\\,{e}^{0}⟩=⟨-1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(0)$ and ${r}^{″}(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨0,\\,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{(1)^{3}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335284 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335749 1069 "q-64780" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335749 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335749 1071 "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335749 1072 #uuid "7b204520-89d9-5ffe-a7f4-e4c68e283388" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335749 1074 "The second-order Cauchy–Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\na{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+bt{y}^{′}+cy=f(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that has the above form is\n\n$$\n2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+4t{y}^{′}-3y=0.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335750 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335657 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335750 1069 "q-64781" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335750 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335750 1071 "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335750 1072 #uuid "926396b9-e4e1-573f-b2ff-a52b442b729c" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335750 1074 "The second-order Cauchy–Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+bx{y}^{′}+cy=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that has the above form is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-2y={x}^{3}{e}^{x}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335751 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335619 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335751 1069 "q-64785" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335751 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335751 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+19x{y}^{′}+81y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335751 1072 #uuid "0d0baceb-8850-5507-a962-183cfbdfc087" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335751 1074 "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+19x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+81{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+19\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+81{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+19\\lambda +81] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+19\\lambda +81 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+18\\lambda +81 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +9)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =-9.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-9}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335752 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335644 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335752 1069 "q-64791" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335752 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335752 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335752 1072 #uuid "b232c50e-a397-5993-a9da-a364656f5e0e" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335752 1074 "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+4x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+2{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+4\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+2{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+4\\lambda +2] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+4\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =-1.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186335753 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335668 13194139533425 175