=⟨-1,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{BC} & =c-b \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩-⟨2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{BA}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{BA}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{BC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{BA}||\\overset{\\to }{BC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{12}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)={0}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336015 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335256 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336015 1069 "q-69831" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336015 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336015 1071 "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(2,1,2)$, $Q(1,3,2)$, and $R(4,5,-2).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336015 1072 #uuid "738d368b-e30a-5573-8091-e990b5e1acb8" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336015 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨2,1,2⟩,\\,q=⟨1,3,2⟩,\\,r=⟨4,5,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$\n\n![](images/30/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨1,3,2⟩-⟨2,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨4,5,-2⟩-⟨2,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 4+0\\cdot (-4)}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5})≈{63}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335641 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336016 1069 "q-69833" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336016 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336016 1071 "Find $∠XZY$ given the points $X(-1,0,3)$, $Y(-2,2,3)$, and $Z(-2,-1,3).$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336016 1072 #uuid "1615350f-44ad-5e4b-849c-6c410f4c58d1" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336016 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $X$, $Y$, $Z$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=⟨-1,0,3⟩,\\,y=⟨-2,2,3⟩,\\,z=⟨-2,-1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△XYZ$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠XZY=\\theta .$ \n\n![](images/87/87453fecb9da6b9e673ab5a85a289a3fac73f4fb90fc8f1b33e7c0a80b1ea77f.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{ZX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{ZY}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ZX} & =x-z \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,3⟩-⟨-2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{ZY} & =y-z \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,3⟩-⟨-2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{ZX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{ZY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{ZX}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{ZY}}{|\\overset{\\to }{ZX}||\\overset{\\to }{ZY}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={45}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335587 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336017 1069 "q-69834" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336017 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336017 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\n- $M$ is a singular matrix\n- $det(M)=1$\n- $det(M)=0$\n\n \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336017 1072 #uuid "924829bc-4eac-54c3-82c8-f773955ca97e" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336017 1074 "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-4-12)+2(-2+6)+1(4+4) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-16)+2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-16+8+8 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335313 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336018 1069 "q-69858" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336018 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336018 1071 "Calculate the minor of the entry ${d}_{33}$ in the matrix\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 54 \\\\ 11 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336018 1072 #uuid "7d92e738-19d1-5e24-b2a0-72ff3b0a1f80" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336018 1074 "The minor of ${d}_{33}=7$ (third row, third column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $7$ are removed: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & ∗ \\\\ 4 & -1 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${d}_{33}=7$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-3-(-8) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336019 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335322 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336019 1069 "q-69891" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336019 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336019 1071 "![](images/9e/9eb144bd4109846f3e1af4fb7d8ed70d2d8a29c3804547b89238d6437c4258d8.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336019 1072 #uuid "e2872275-2ccf-570a-873b-56e8f9665a65" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336019 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $b=1.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335596 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336020 1069 "q-69892" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336020 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336020 1071 "![](images/1a/1acc2d6b8387947ff7186322796e273bcec366810ad73ac0025494ab627e06b2.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336020 1072 #uuid "daa08063-18f2-5c3f-bfa5-03e9e47d2153" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336020 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=1$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336021 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335347 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336021 1069 "q-69893" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336021 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336021 1071 "![](images/be/be8468d84e3820bd1e9181d31aa3a1b7d1a85c743020a69cb67fc3f601a88363.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336021 1072 #uuid "cc59b788-b5c5-5dc7-bac3-9ab39eac60d6" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336021 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=5.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336022 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335676 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336022 1069 "q-69899" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336022 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336022 1071 "Which of the following numbers lie in the second quadrant in an Argand diagram?\n\n- $-2+\\text{i}$\n- $-3-2\\text{i}$\n- $-3+2\\text{i}$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336022 1072 #uuid "36192a12-3447-5ff2-9e37-3b54bb995a4a" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336022 1074 "- The complex number $-2+\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=1.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,1)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-3-2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-3$ and the imaginary part $y=-2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-3,-2)$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-3+2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-3$ and the imaginary part $y=2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-3,2)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n![](images/d6/d683b808bee4b7893952adbaf87940066f9ff7d61c99ce7cc7e05fc9a4fbe02f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336023 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335466 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336023 1069 "q-69903" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336023 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336023 1071 "$\\frac{2}{x(1+z)}+\\frac{1}{3(1+z)}=$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336023 1072 #uuid "45828168-91a4-5191-ae19-572992805b62" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336023 1074 "In this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of $3x(1+z),$ since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit.\n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $3$, and the top and bottom of the second fraction by $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x(1+z)}+\\frac{1}{3(1+z)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{x(1+z)}+\\frac{x}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3(1+z)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{3x(1+z)}+\\frac{x}{3x(1+z)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6+x}{3x(1+z)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336024 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335381 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336024 1069 "q-69907" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336024 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336024 1071 "Find the second derivative of $f(t)={e}^{3t+2}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j-2{t}^{5}\\,k$ with respect to $t.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336024 1072 #uuid "f2ea5cad-9652-5b52-b71f-3a11074a333d" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336024 1074 "First, we find the derivative of $f(t)$ by differentiating each component.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t+2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{t}^{5})\\,k \\\\  & =3{e}^{3t+2}\\,i+(-2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,j+(-2\\cdot 5{t}^{4})\\,k \\\\  & =3{e}^{3t+2}\\,i-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j-10{t}^{4}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThen, we calculate ${f}^{″}(t)$ by differentiating ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{e}^{3t+2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-10{t}^{4})\\,k \\\\  & =3(3{e}^{3t+2})\\,i-2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,j-10(4{t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =9{e}^{3t+2}\\,i-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j-40{t}^{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336025 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335595 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336025 1069 "q-69922" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336025 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336025 1071 "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=2t+1,\\,\\,\\,y=2t-1,\\,0\\le t\\le 5,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $b$ | $7$ | $9$ | $11$ |\n| $y$ | $a$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $c$ | $9$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a-b-c.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336025 1072 #uuid "cc1d2bf2-3dfe-5506-8434-072c2dc13cdb" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336025 1074 "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=0$ into $y=2t-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=2(0)-1=0-1=-1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=2$ into $x=2t+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=2(2)+1=4+1=5.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=4$ into $y=2t-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=2(4)-1=8-1=7.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na-b-c=-1-5-7=-13.\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336026 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335300 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336026 1069 "q-69938" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336026 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336026 1071 "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=⟨-2,3⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨4,-6⟩.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336026 1072 #uuid "7214ad71-6f54-5736-979a-1f4039dc6fdd" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336026 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-6)-4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12-12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336027 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335531 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336027 1069 "q-69940" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336027 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336027 1071 "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336027 1072 #uuid "6e1b0899-7688-507f-be9e-fc4727af5605" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336027 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-2+2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336028 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335635 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336028 1069 "q-69941" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336028 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336028 1071 "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336028 1072 #uuid "2ec973be-811c-5c98-ab7e-a27196c22154" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336028 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336029 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335691 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336029 1069 "q-69943" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336029 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336029 1071 "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336029 1072 #uuid "a101db17-b08e-5c17-8786-6539ae6cbc0e" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336029 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336030 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335421 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336030 1069 "q-69945" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336030 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336030 1071 "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=⟨-1,-2⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨3,6⟩.$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336030 1072 #uuid "92767c82-7016-540a-a963-df010d8da1b2" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336030 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 6-3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-6+6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335419 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336031 1069 "q-69946" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336031 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336031 1071 "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336031 1072 #uuid "16ddeea5-cbc1-599e-ac3c-ae0bcf472762" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336031 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 4 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 4 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336032 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335353 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336032 1069 "q-69952" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336032 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336032 1071 "$|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 11 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|=$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336032 1072 #uuid "bf461bb2-5354-5d4c-a70d-475dbba2d043" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336032 1074 "The $4$th row of the matrix contains three zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 11 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{41}+(-2)\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+0\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 8\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336033 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335654 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336033 1069 "q-69962" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336033 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336033 1071 "Express $z=\\frac{10}{-3+4\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336033 1072 #uuid "64737006-09f0-5f7b-b3c3-c7aaff447f72" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336033 1074 "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $-3-4\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{10}{-3+4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(-3-4\\text{i})}{((-3)+4\\text{i})((-3)-4\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(-3-4\\text{i})}{(-3)^{2}-16{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-30-40\\text{i}}{9+16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{30}{25}-\\frac{40}{25}\\text{i} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{8}{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=-\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{8}{5}\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{8}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{6}^{2}+{8}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{100}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=-\\frac{6}{5}$ and $y=-\\frac{8}{5}$ are both negative, $z$ lies in the third quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{(-\\frac{8}{5})}{(-\\frac{6}{5})}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & ≈4.069,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4.069)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4.069))\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336034 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335521 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336034 1069 "q-69966" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336034 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336034 1071 "Express $z=\\frac{4}{1+2\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336034 1072 #uuid "2ba9feb4-93ae-58b8-887d-a3260d202f9b" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true] [17592186336034 1074 "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1-2\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{4}{1+2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-2\\text{i})}{(1+2\\text{i})(1-2\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-2\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-2\\text{i})}{1+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{8}{5}\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{8}{5}\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{8}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{80}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16\\cdot 5}{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ is positive and $y=-\\frac{8}{5}$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{(-\\frac{8}{5})}{(\\frac{4}{5})}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡2 \\\\  & ≈5.176,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.176)).\n$$" 13194139533425 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-000286c7-5d78-5f85-8582-341603e6a0f7" 17592186332994 "answer-0035afd4-f801-529f-8c26-e89eab48fce0" 17592186332995 "answer-003dd8c0-effa-5c3f-8dd0-7ffa4f3958d5" 17592186332996 "answer-00496f44-b185-5137-9471-f034eecb6b21" 17592186332997 "answer-008964b2-7dcd-5496-839e-a3936e4e413e" 17592186332998 "answer-009a70ce-f969-523c-9a1f-7fa8409b9ad7" 17592186332999 "answer-00a8dc82-7cf5-5eb6-9cb4-cc65873162f8" 17592186333000 "answer-00b7f2f0-c558-5016-9142-f0f14f81022c" 17592186333001 "answer-00c08cbe-3db3-5a02-8a65-ca8e636b6ad2" 17592186333002 "answer-00d79063-6591-5f56-83fc-3f10aab147de" 17592186333003 "answer-010d251c-f20d-5864-b8af-b4f7c6f6ccfc" 17592186333004 "answer-01216287-0f50-583c-9086-c9c0e4cc5138" 17592186333005 "answer-013bdb1e-e69a-5bae-a13